### The parent questions whether the adults or setting can give their child appropriate care.

15 of 140 posts.

Parents of children aged two to nearly four question daily negative reports, harsh or joyless expectations, staff turnover, inadequate help and whether withdrawal signals a problem in the child or the setting. Some have already changed or left care; others are considering smaller care after withdrawal or continued distress, or asking what new fears and subdued peers mean. Reports include withheld recess, a nursery not calling during prolonged crying, ignored requests for comfort or water, dismissal for not joining the group and concern about an old teacher seeking information after a move.

- "So I pulled him. I just didn’t feel good continuing to send him somewhere that didn’t seem willing (or able) to meet him where he is. My biggest fear is him starting to think he’s a “bad kid.”" [ar029-0019]
- "The nursery leader said she just sat by the door crying almost the entire time.  I asked why she didn’t come get me and she said “I know you need a break and need that time too so I didn’t want to bother you, I kept thinking she would be done crying soon”" [ar074-0058]
- "I'm so angry right now. Ignoring toddler who can't take care of themselves is bad enough, but not giving them water in the summer heat is flat out neglect." [ar004-0107]
- "She got kicked out of school in April, because,  to no one's shock,  she didn't want to sit with the group as an almost 3 year old and do what everyone else is doing." [ar058-0059]
- "It all adds up to my daughter basically puttering around with some household and/or wooden objects she doesn't seem all that interested in waiting to get told she's doing it wrong and asking me to go home. Is it really meant to be this joyless?" [ar034-0118]
- "Constant negative feedback is a problem and the way it is communicated is itself another one. Feedback is frequently given with 0 context on what happened before." [ar040-0099]
- "Apparently the teacher from the old center - the one my child just didn’t vibe with - was texting other providers in town asking where we went." [ar028-0116]

**But:** A change of provider can be followed by a very different response from the child. "So far it has been great! My son is happy in the morning and asks to go see his new provider. He has even asked to go there over the weekend and talks about her nonstop." [ar028-0115]

