### The parent wants clearer information about what happens while their child is in someone else's care.

3 of 140 posts.

Parents of two-year-olds want information about peer incidents, food, naps, bowel movements, daily activities and what classes are meant to teach. One child reports biting and dirt throwing without a corresponding teacher report; another comes home sad while the parent receives only a general reassurance. A parent attending Montessori daycare, swimming and dance describes little explanation across all three and repeated requests to restore care logs.

- "I’m more concerned on why I wasn’t told about this from the teacher. Is this not something the teacher should be reporting to parents?" [ar013-0002]
- "Daycare doesn’t give update throughout the day, I honestly have no idea what they do exactly besides eat, go outside, and dance to music. At pickup, daycare just tells us she did good. We have to ask if she ate or nap." [ar058-0110]
- "No initial conversation about the nature of the class on the first day.  No post-lesson conversation about things to practice or advice for individual children.  Just right in, right out." [ar070-0157]
- "For daycare, I have asked the administrators multiple times to get the BM and nap notes back at the bare minimum.  They said they would, but clearly aren't taking it that seriously." [ar070-0163]

**But:** The parent seeking an incident report explicitly leaves room for uncertainty in the toddler's account. "I am keeping the possibility that her account of what happened is different than what actually happened, since like you said, someone that age is not the best narrator." [ar013-0003]

