# baby-development: pattern 9, Classes, groups, other children, daycare as a place to grow (children of 2 and 3 years)

**Written:** 2026-11-20 from baby-development/evidence/2026-11/age-reread/digest-11.md
**Reviewed by the owner:** not yet

## Limits

A keyword-free random sample covered 20,000 Reddit posts from twelve parenting subreddits, 1 April–24 October 2026; 3,811 were on-niche, including 1,340 about children of two and three. This pattern has 140 on-niche and 38 near posts, all read; counts are posts tagged by one reader, not people or market prevalence. Merging forms across readers requires judgement; this single digest’s distinct forms remain separate.

## On-niche posts

### Parents want easier daycare goodbyes.

21 of 140 posts.

Distress sometimes persists.

- "He can walk into the building by himself, excitedly exclaim “friends!” while walking towards his classroom, but when it comes to crossing the threshold, he won’t do it." [ar037-0054]
- "Parents always say "oh it will get better in a few weeks" i turn and say "its been going on 2 years"." [ar012-0086]

**But:** "Twice this week he ran ahead of me." [ar012-0060]

### Parents question inadequate care.

15 of 140 posts.

Reports include neglect and expulsion.

- "Ignoring toddler who can't take care of themselves is bad enough, but not giving them water in the summer heat is flat out neglect." [ar004-0107]
- "Is it really meant to be this joyless?" [ar034-0118]

**But:** "My son is happy in the morning and asks to go see his new provider." [ar028-0115]

### Parents seek suitable schools.

14 of 140 posts.

Safety and values matter.

- "How can I be sure that she won’t get abused at daycare?" [ar005-0079]
- "One place genuinely told me ‘our primary focus is spreading the gospel to the children’. 🤦‍♀️" [ar048-0133]

**But:** "My toddler loves it and learns a lot from circle time." [ar011-0132]

### Parents question distressing classes.

12 of 140 posts.

Fear competes with persistence.

- "I just worry if that will create a child that won’t finish anything they started." [ar049-0025]
- "The instructor and Aquatics Manager at the location both advised sticking with it saying she needs to face her fears, insisting on submersions, but it ends up feeling like we're dunking her against her will like water boarding." [ar018-0040]

**But:** "So the lesson ended and we stayed for another hour or so because again, SHE LOVES THE POOL." [ar054-0042]

### Parents weigh preschool against home.

11 of 140 posts.

Attendance levels remain uncertain.

- "I've heard it said here that pre-k/daycare is beneficial starting at 3 but is there any research on how much is beneficial?" [ar082-0030]
- "I'm wondering if there is an age where the benefits of going to school and being among peers 5 days a week would outweigh the benefits we are getting now from the part-time arrangement?" [ar079-0051]

**But:** Readiness is uncertain. "I have no concerns about his “academics”, I think the only real benefit from school for him would be the social-emotional aspect, \*but\* I don’t know if he is socially and emotionally ready for it." [ar003-0072]

### Parents ask about school preparation.

10 of 140 posts.

Transitions require preparation.

- "We've been practicing opening and using the lunchbox he'll take and opening/closing his water bottle." [ar050-0027]
- "Do we make it no big deal or do we start talking about it to prepare him?" [ar061-0051]

**But:** "We took a tour of a different daycare (we took our daughter with us and she didn’t want to leave!)" [ar070-0135]

### Parents want protection from rough children.

10 of 140 posts.

Intervention raises questions.

- "The boy said it was his playground and he could punch anyone in the face." [ar046-0020]
- "She said she told the boy “no” and pulled away repeatedly." [ar007-0057]

**But:** Parents may feel more hurt. "I was the only one being offended." [ar061-0108]

### Parents question lessons’ value.

8 of 140 posts.

Swimming dominates these questions.

- "What can you expect a 3 year old to learn at this age?" [ar074-0055]
- "Is there any evidence that ISR reduces drowning risk more than standard lessons, or that skills learned at this age actually stick?" [ar044-0051]

**But:** "In the meantime id plan to take her to the community pool and recreate the lessons to keep water confidence up." [ar061-0016]

### Parents work to arrange friendships.

7 of 140 posts.

Contact requires organising.

- "Do we ask for the parents' contact information and arrange a playdate?" [ar022-0092]
- "She doesn’t really have “friends” because she doesn’t go to daycare or school yet." [ar010-0099]

**But:** "My 2 y/o is in daycare 40 hours a week and gets a lot of social time there." [ar041-0130]

### Parents want sociability without pressure.

5 of 140 posts.

Settings affect participation.

- "It’s very hard to get her to do anything in public spaces, so I am afraid that it’s impacting her learning." [ar005-0057]
- "I’ve tried to do some different activities with him but he never wants to participate or even interact with other kids." [ar047-0046]

**But:** "On the other side of the family, she has 2 around her age and she is quite social." [ar069-0033]

### Parents want sharing alongside boundaries.

5 of 140 posts.

Ownership complicates kindness.

- "I want my kid to be a good sharer." [ar070-0103]
- "But I also want him to stand up for himself if a kid takes a toy he's playing with." [ar070-0103]

**But:** "we don't share our toys we bring to the pool." [ar070-0104]

### Parents worry about exclusion.

5 of 140 posts.

Rejection worries them.

- "This only happens when all of three of them meet, their behaviour is completely different and accepting when they're solo." [ar025-0047]
- "I’m also worried that he considers someone who is mean to him “a friend”…" [ar080-0056]

**But:** Rejection can alternate with affection. "Sarah will appear kind and ask to hold hands and at this point my daughter is like "no" coz she is so confused." [ar076-0092]

### Parents weigh classmates against teachers.

4 of 140 posts.

Stimulation also matters.

- "The primary 3s room has a much better teacher in it." [ar019-0124]
- "I also wonder whether being one of the oldest will be less stimulating for her." [ar079-0118]

**But:** "They said she wasn’t gaining independence at school." [ar036-0074]

### Parents want clearer caregiver reports.

3 of 140 posts.

General reassurance is insufficient.

- "Is this not something the teacher should be reporting to parents?" [ar013-0002]
- "At pickup, daycare just tells us she did good." [ar058-0110]

**But:** Toddlers’ accounts remain uncertain. "I am keeping the possibility that her account of what happened is different than what actually happened, since like you said, someone that age is not the best narrator." [ar013-0003]

### Parents want cooperation with groups.

3 of 140 posts.

Group expectations differ.

- "Got a feedback from his teachers that he is great 1:1 but very difficult when he is given group-directed instructions." [ar001-0058]
- "He doesn’t follow the class structure, do what the instructor says to do, he wants to do his own thing." [ar058-0026]

**But:** Hitting and spitting improved. "That has since subsided mostly." [ar073-0086]

### Parents appreciate school support.

2 of 140 posts.

Two parents praise Montessori support.

